Step 9 — Validate functionality and performance
Before deciding whether this can replace DCV for any users, test the following:
Test	What to verify
Initial connection	Login screen appears consistently
AD authentication	Domain users can log in using the expected credentials
Session isolation	Different users do not share an unintended desktop session
Application launch	Your actual simulation, labelling, and VS Code workflows work
Keyboard and mouse	No unusual input delay
Screen updates	Scrolling, window movement, and rendering remain responsive
Disconnect and reconnect	Session behaviour is acceptable and does not lose work unexpectedly
Reboot	RDP becomes available again after a reboot

Run the same workload through DCV and GNOME RDP under comparable conditions. Record connection latency, CPU/GPU usage, and any input or display freezes.
Troubleshooting
RDP connection is refused

Check the listener, service logs, security group, subnet routing, and UFW rules.
sudo ss -lntp | grep ':3389'
sudo journalctl -u gnome-remote-desktop.service -n 100 --no-pager



Connection succeeds, but no login screen appears

Check whether the installed GNOME Remote Desktop package supports system-level Remote Login, whether GDM is running, and whether the system RDP backend is enabled.

Linux login works, but AD login fails

Check SSSD, PAM, GDM, and the existing domain-login configuration. A working SSH login does not by itself prove that graphical login is configured correctly.

The desktop opens but performance is poor

Check the graphical session, GPU acceleration, display resolution, and application workload. GNOME RDP may not improve performance compared with DCV.


